== 3.1 Medicines and Responsible Use

A *drug* is a substance that can affect body or mental processes. A *medicine* is used for a medical purpose such as prevention, diagnosis or treatment. Medicines can be helpful, but their effects and risks depend on the substance, dose, route, timing and individual circumstances. A medicine prescribed for one person is not automatically suitable for another.

Follow a qualified prescriber's or pharmacist's instructions and the product label. Ask questions about dosage, interactions, storage and what to do after a missed dose. Do not share prescription medicines or change a prescribed course on the basis of an advertisement or another person's experience. Consult a qualified professional if a medicine causes a concerning reaction; severe reactions may require urgent assistance.

Antibiotics act against susceptible bacteria; they do not treat viral infections such as the common cold. Unnecessary or inappropriate antibiotic use contributes to antimicrobial resistance. A clinician decides whether an antibiotic is appropriate and which regimen to use. Do not keep or share leftover antibiotics as a substitute for assessment.

#rule-box("Prescription is personal")[
  The same symptoms can have different causes, and medicines may interact with other medicines or conditions. A label or another person's prescription is not a diagnosis.
]

== 3.2 Alcohol, Tobacco and Other Substances

Alcohol can impair judgement, reaction time and coordination, increasing the risk of injury and unsafe decisions. Effects depend on factors including amount, rate of consumption and the person. Avoid driving or operating machinery after drinking. Alcohol can interact with medicines; seek qualified advice rather than assuming a combination is safe. A person who is difficult to wake or has slow or abnormal breathing after drinking needs urgent help.

Tobacco smoke contains harmful chemicals and exposure increases the risk of illnesses including cardiovascular and respiratory disease and cancer. Second-hand smoke can also harm other people. Nicotine can cause dependence. Vaping products and other nicotine products are not harmless, particularly for young people; their risks and regulation vary. Support for stopping tobacco use can involve counselling and, where appropriate, medicines recommended by a qualified professional.

Other substances can affect alertness, breathing, judgement or behaviour. An unknown product may contain a different substance or strength than expected, and combining substances can increase risk. Never assume that a product is safe because it is sold informally, labelled herbal or used by someone else.

