== 3.3 Dependence, Support and Stigma

Dependence can involve a strong drive to use a substance and difficulty controlling use, sometimes alongside tolerance or withdrawal. Experiences differ, and not every person who uses a substance meets criteria for a disorder. A diagnosis and a care plan require appropriate professional assessment. Some withdrawal syndromes can be dangerous: do not advise a person to stop a substance abruptly without considering their circumstances and obtaining qualified advice.

Supportive, non-judgemental communication can help people seek care. Treatment and recovery options differ by substance and individual. Structural factors, availability, stress and social context also matter; a simple claim that dependence is merely a lack of willpower is misleading. A trusted adult, health professional or appropriate support service can help a person explore options.

#concept-box("Pressure and refusal")[
  A learner can state a clear refusal, leave an unsafe situation and seek support from a trusted person. Safety planning should account for the person's circumstances rather than assume every situation is equally easy to leave.
]

== 3.4 Emergencies and Information

If a person is unresponsive, has difficulty breathing or has severe symptoms after possible substance exposure, contact local emergency services immediately. Follow the dispatcher's instructions, avoid exposing yourself to danger and provide any known information about the substance. Do not force food, drink or medication into an unresponsive person. If trained, provide first aid within your training while help is on the way; responses depend on the person's condition and local guidance.

Check health claims against reliable, current sources and ask an appropriately qualified professional. This chapter is educational, not a treatment protocol or an individual risk assessment.

== 3.5 Four Worked Examples
#worked-example(1, "A medicine", tier: "FOUNDATION",
  [A medicine can be used for \
A. prevention, diagnosis or treatment \
B. guaranteeing every person has the same response \
C. replacing all qualified assessment \
D. proving that side effects cannot occur],
  [Medicines have medical purposes, but their benefits and risks depend on appropriate use and individual circumstances. The answer is *A*.])
#worked-example(2, "Antibiotics", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [A friend offers leftover antibiotics for a common cold. What is the most appropriate response? \
A. Take them because all colds are bacterial \
B. Decline and seek appropriate advice if needed \
C. Share them with several classmates \
D. Assume any dose is suitable],
  [Common colds are viral; antibiotics do not treat viruses. Do not use another person's leftover prescription. The answer is *B*.])
#worked-example(3, "Impaired judgement", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [Why is driving after drinking alcohol unsafe? \
A. Alcohol guarantees faster reactions \
B. Alcohol has no effect on coordination \
C. Alcohol can impair judgement and reaction time \
D. A familiar substance is always harmless],
  [Alcohol can impair the abilities needed for safe driving and increase injury risk. The answer is *C*.])
#worked-example(4, "An emergency", tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
  [A person is unresponsive and breathing abnormally after a possible drug exposure. What is the priority? \
A. Give food and wait \
B. Force a drink \
C. Leave the person alone \
D. Call local emergency services and follow the dispatcher's instructions],
  [Unresponsiveness and abnormal breathing are emergencies. Call for help, protect your own safety and follow dispatcher guidance. Do not force anything into the person's mouth. The answer is *D*.])

#trap-box("Safe is not the same as legal")[
  A legal product can still cause harm; a medicine can be beneficial when properly used and harmful when misused. Avoid both blanket assumptions and stigma.
]

== 3.6 Two-Minute Set
#mcq(1, [A prescribed medicine should generally be], [used as instructed for the person it was prescribed for], [shared whenever symptoms look similar], [changed solely after an advertisement], [taken at any dose])
#mcq(2, [Antibiotics do not treat], [susceptible bacterial infections], [viral infections such as the common cold], [some bacterial infections], [bacterial infections when the organism is susceptible])
#mcq(3, [Second-hand tobacco smoke], [harms only the person smoking], [has no health effect], [can harm people exposed to it], [is a form of clean air])
#mcq(4, [An unresponsive person with abnormal breathing needs], [food forced into the mouth], [no assistance], [a private diagnosis from a friend], [urgent help and dispatcher-guided action])
#answer-key-grid(("A", "B", "C", "D"))
#answer-notes((("A", "Prescriptions are individual; consult a professional about questions or reactions."), ("B", "Antibiotics do not act against viruses."), ("C", "Second-hand smoke can harm exposed people."), ("D", "Unresponsiveness and abnormal breathing require urgent help.")))
#concept-box("Recap card")[
  Medicines have benefits and risks. Alcohol can impair judgement; tobacco smoke harms smokers and others. Dependence deserves informed, non-stigmatising care. Severe symptoms require urgent help.
]
